::: {.doc-subject}

Add a complete graph (clique) to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L8099-L8234){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_full_graph(
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
    keep_loops=False,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Complete graph](../assets/graphs/add-full-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three complete graphs side by side: a triangle for n=3, a square with both diagonals for n=4, and a hexagon with every chord for n=6. Every pair of nodes is joined, giving 3, 6, and 15 edges." width="100%"}

Creates `n` nodes where every node is connected to every other node. How many edges that
takes depends on the graph's directedness:

- undirected graphs get one edge per pair: `n(n - 1) / 2` edges
- directed graphs get an edge in each direction for every pair: `n(n - 1)` edges

With `keep_loops=True`, each node also gets a self-loop, adding `n` more edges. The new
nodes are appended to the graph without touching or connecting to existing nodes.

Complete graphs are the densest possible simple graphs, which makes them useful for:

- modeling groups in which everyone interacts with everyone (a team, a committee)
- creating dense cliques to plant inside sparser graphs, e.g. to test community or clique
  detection
- checking edge cases, since every node has the maximum possible degree of `n - 1`

The edge count grows quadratically, so large values of `n=` produce very many edges
(`n=1000` already gives about half a million in an undirected graph).
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the clique. Must be at least `1`; smaller values raise a
    `ValueError`. With `n=1` a single node is added with no edges (unless `keep_loops=` is
    `True`).

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string (so `"1"`,
    `"2"`, ... in an empty graph); a label that is already used raises `ValueError` before
    anything is added. If `False`, the nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge, including any
    self-loops. If `None` (default), no `rel` attribute is set.

<code>[keep_loops]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True`, also add a self-loop on every new node, adding `n` edges and making the
    graph non-simple. Default is `False` (no self-loops).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `n=` is less than `1`.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_complete_multipartite_graph`) 

:   Add a complete multipartite graph.

[](`~add_turan_graph`) 

:   Add a Turán graph.

[](`~clique_number`) 

:   Compute the size of a largest clique.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A complete graph joins every pair of nodes, so `n` nodes have `n(n - 1) / 2` edges. In a
directed graph each pair is joined both ways, which doubles the count:

::: {#bc8c5bf9 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

(
    ec.Graph().add_full_graph(n=4).count_edges(),
    ec.Graph(directed=True).add_full_graph(n=4).count_edges(),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(6, 12)
```
:::
:::


`keep_loops=True` adds a self-loop on every node, so the graph is no longer simple:

::: {#c19299ca .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
looped = ec.Graph().add_full_graph(n=3, keep_loops=True)

looped.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True), looped.is_simple()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(['1--1', '1--2', '1--3', '2--2', '2--3', '3--3'], False)
```
:::
:::


### Four nodes and eight

The number of edges grows with the square of the number of nodes: 6 edges for the 4 nodes
on the left, 28 for the 8 on the right:

::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#06346663 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
ec.Graph().add_full_graph(n=4).layout_circular().plot(
    layout="pos", label_by="label", width=320, height=320, legend=False
)
```

